<section class="band band--night">
  <div class="shell">
    <h2>What changes when you are licensed</h2>
    <p class="intro-wide">Supervision is the only part of this with an end date. Here is what
    happens to the rest of it on the day the Council grants your LPC.</p>
    <dl class="facts facts--wide">
      <div>
        <dt>Supervision</dt>
        <dd>Ends, and the monthly fee ends with it. There is nothing further to pay and nothing
        further to file.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>EMDR consultation</dt>
        <dd>Goes from free to $185 an hour, and keeps
        counting toward EMDRIA certification if that is still in progress. The consultant is
        already the person who knows your caseload.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Ethics hours</dt>
        <dd>Become a recurring obligation rather than an occasional one: 24 CE hours each
        renewal period, six of them ethics, and at least half from a provider listed in 22 TAC
        &sect;681.140(f).</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>The retreat</dt>
        <dd>Stays open. It is for interns, associates and licensed therapists, and depletion
        does not stop at licensure.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="shell band">
  <h2>How to apply</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Schedule the free thirty-minute consult</strong> by Zoom or phone. Email
    <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}">{{ site.email }}</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Send your CV or resume and three professional references</strong>, along with
    your answers to the questions below.</li>
    <li><strong>Verification, then signing.</strong> References and site verification are
    required before a supervision agreement is signed.</li>
    <li><strong>The Supervisory Agreement Form is filed</strong> before supervision starts.
    It is one of the <a href="/texas-lpc-licensure/">deadlines associates most often miss</a>,
    and it is handled here rather than left to you to remember.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Include your answers to these in your email</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>What inspired you to become a therapist?</li>
    <li>What do you hope to gain from supervision at this stage?</li>
    <li>Which approaches or populations are you most drawn to?</li>
    <li>How do you care for yourself as a helping professional?</li>
    <li>What kind of feedback helps you grow best?</li>
    <li>Are you interested in equine-assisted or mindfulness-based supervision?</li>
    <li>Are you interested in building your own private practice?</li>
  </ol>
